ave 10 points. Each line can cover at most some number of points. Non-sunny lines can be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. Let's consider possible non-sunny lines:\n\n- Vertical lines: x=1, x=2, x=3, x=4. Each vertical line covers points in that column: x=1 covers 4 points, x=2 covers 3 points, x=3 covers 2 points, x=4 covers 1 point.\n\n- Horizontal lines: y=1, y=2, y=3, y=4. Horizontal y=1 covers points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1) = 4 points. y=2 covers (1,2),(2,2),(3,2) = 3 points. y=3 covers (1,3),(2,3) = 2 points. y=4 covers (1,4) = 1 point.\n\n- Slope -1 lines: x+y = c for c from 2 to 5 (since a+b ≤5). For c=2: (1,1) only. c=3: (1,2),(2,1) = 2 points. c=4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) = 3 points. c=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) = 4 points.\n\nThus the largest non-sunny line can cover up to 4 points (vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, slope -1 line x+y=5). So each non-sunny line can cover at most 4 points.\n\nSunny lines can be any other slope. The maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is maybe 2 or 3? Let's check possible sunny lines for n=4.\n\nWe can try to find a sunny line that passes through many points. For slope 1 (y = x + c), we can have c = 0: y = x passes through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3?) but (3,3) not in region because 3+3=6 >5. So only (1,1),(2,2). That's 2 points. c = -1: y = x - 1 passes through (2,1),(3,2) (both in region). That's 2 points. c = 1: y = x + 1 passes through (1,2),(2,3) (both in region). That's 2 points. c = -2: y = x - 2 passes through (3,1) only (since (2,0) not allowed). So at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 2: y = 2x + c. For c = -1: y = 2x - 1 passes through (1,1),(2,3). That's 2 points. c = -2: y = 2x - 2 passes through (1,0) not allowed, (2,2) allowed, (3,4) not allowed. So only (2,2). So at most 2 points.\n\nSlope -2: y = -2x + c. For c = 5: y = -2x + 5 passes through (2,1),(3,-1) not allowed. So only (2,1). For c = 6: y = -2x + 6 passes through (2,2),(3,0) not allowed. So only (2,2). For c = 7: passes (2,3),(3,1). Both allowed. So line y = -2x + 7 passes